cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "TIRI — Relevance screening of scientific literature against a research brief"
message: >-
  If you refer to this work, please cite it using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Fossier
    given-names: Sylvain
    alias: Sylvain-gitty
  - family-names: Fauvel
    given-names: Warren
    alias: warrenof
repository-code: "https://github.com/Sylvain-gitty/TIRI---The-Data-science-project"
abstract: >-
  TIRI turns a labelled corpus of academic literature into a working relevance-screening
  model, and documents what four weeks of measurement showed about the approach. Its central
  finding is that relevance is a property of the (brief, paper) pair rather than of the paper
  alone: a classifier trained on paper-only embedding features recovers which research
  question a paper belongs to at 96.2% accuracy, so a model pooled across questions learns
  question identity rather than relevance and collapses to roughly chance on an unseen
  question. Ten brief-relative scalars beat a full 384-dimension embedding on the same test,
  and with zero labels, ranking by plain cosine similarity to the brief beats every
  supervised cross-question model trained here. The repository carries the resulting
  per-question architecture, an external validation at realistic (about 2%) prevalence, and a
  register of measured negative results.
keywords:
  - systematic review screening
  - technology intelligence
  - information retrieval
  - text classification
  - sentence embeddings
  - ensemble learning
  - reproducible research
  - negative results
date-released: "2026-08-19"
version: "1.0.0"
